    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i get the math part. but can you walk me through a practical example? say we use 500 units a day on average, our lead time is usually 14 days but sometimes it takes 20, and the standard dev for lead time is 3 days. if i use your formula, how much extra stock should i actually be holding to be safe? give me a specific number for the safety stock so i can plug it into my spreadsheet.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Assuming you aim for a 95% service level (a Z-score of 1.645), we first need the standard deviation of demand during lead time, which requires knowing demand variability. If we simplify and focus on just the lead time uncertainty, the safety stock calculation will be based on the variability of the 14-day cycle shifting to a 20-day cycle. For a concrete number, if the standard deviation of *demand* daily is, say, 20 units, the safety stock would be roughly $1.645 \\times \\sqrt{(14 \\times 20^2) + (500 \\times 3^2)} \\approx 395$ units.",
      "source": "target",
